      {
        "t": "chr",
        "v": [
          "This module classifies repository files and, for tabular data files available locally, extracts each column's type and summary statistics.",
          "#### Data Tree",
          "Where the files sit within each repository. Each file is followed by how it was classified, which study it belongs to.",
          "**Repository: https://osf.io/rdata**",
          "```\n└── data/\n    ├── analysis_workspace.RData  📊 data ex1\n    ├── clean_data.RData          📊 data ex1\n    └── study.csv                 📊 data ex1\n```",
          "#### Data Files",
          "::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## clean_data.RData\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(id = 1:5, score = c(1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5, 5.5)), row.names = c(NA, \n5L), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 10, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n## study.csv\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(id = 1:5, x = c(0.1, 0.4, 0.2, 0.8, 0.5)), row.names = c(NA, \n5L), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 10, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n:::",
          "#### Descriptives Overview\n\nWe found 0 columns with at least one missing value.",
          "::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## clean_data.RData\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(Column = c(\"id\", \"score\"), Representation = c(\"text\", \n\"numeric\"), Level = c(\"nominal\", \"ratio\"), Concept = c(\"id\", \n\"\"), Role = c(\"identifier\", \"measure\"), Unit = c(\"\", \"\"), Rows = c(5L, \n5L), \"% Missing\" = c(0, 0), \"N Unique\" = c(5L, 5L), Mean = c(NA, \n3.5), SD = c(NA, 1.581), Min = c(NA, 1.5), Max = c(NA, 5.5)), row.names = c(\"id...1\", \n\"score\"), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 25, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n## study.csv\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(Column = c(\"id\", \"x\"), Representation = c(\"text\", \n\"numeric\"), Level = c(\"nominal\", \"ratio\"), Concept = c(\"id\", \n\"\"), Role = c(\"identifier\", \"measure\"), Unit = c(\"\", \"\"), Rows = c(5L, \n5L), \"% Missing\" = c(0, 0), \"N Unique\" = c(5L, 5L), Mean = c(NA, \n0.4), SD = c(NA, 0.274), Min = c(NA, 0.1), Max = c(NA, 0.8)), row.names = c(\"id...2\", \n\"x\"), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 25, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n:::",
          "This module runs automated data-quality checks (outliers, miscoded missing values, empty and constant columns, SPSS filter variables, category casing, problematic column names, mixed text encodings, spreadsheet formatting) on the extracted data files.",
          "We examined 4 columns across 2 data files.",
          "#### Careless Responding",
          "No file could be screened for careless responding.",
          "**What this does not cover.** Only 0 of 2 data files could be screened: the rest had no detectable multi-item scale block, or too few respondents. Even in a screened file, these checks only look at multi-item rating scales — the only place where a repeated or flat answer pattern is interpretable. Single questions, open text, demographics and any item outside a detected scale block are never examined. A respondent can therefore answer most of a survey carelessly and still not be flagged here, and finding nothing is not evidence that a dataset is free of careless responding."
        ]
